er in  a situation like this is to follow an  independent process where someone  independent looks at the Union's  position looks at the government's  position and comes to a fair compromise  and that's what we think should happen  and that's why we are supporting the  independent pay review body processes  what more or less would you say the rate  of wage inflation is for for the private  sector and the public sector at the  moment  well um  for this year with the independent pay  review body we agreed with a pay rise  for nurses which is comparable to the  private sector and just specifically at  the moment what you know what kind of  Gap is there would you say at the moment  between private sector and public sector  well I've just said for this year if you  look at the private sector pay Rises uh  four to five percent and we agree to a  five and a half percent rise for nurses  next year the independent payer view  body will look at those factors and they  will come to a balanced judgment and we  are happy to follow that independent  process we think that there is a genuine  difficulty in coming to the right  balance between our concern not to lock  in high inflation and people's worries  about how far their pay is going and we  think an independent process is the  right solution but the answer to answer  is that the private sector pays only  about seven percent public sector pay is  running at about two percent or less  than two percent if you look at some  measures there is an enormous Gap that's  opening up now so can you not see why a  lot of people particularly in the public  sector are so frustrated well I don't  say for a moment that these aren't very  sincerely held positions and surely the  right answer this is not fair for them  to get closer to to the private sector  rate of inflation they're falling back  and these are everyone is getting poorer  right now but they are getting poorer  faster than the private sector well the  right answer in that situation then is  to follow an independent process that  comes to a fair balance between the  worries people have about high inflation  affecting their pay packet and the  government's concern not to lock in high  inflation even if that independent  process is out of date which you know  that happened months ago things have  changed since then inflation's got  higher well so will you not go back and  look at the numbers and say maybe that  needs to be reflected well we have  accepted in the case of the nurses the  independent peer review bodies  recommendations for this year which was  back in the summer  as far as next year is concerned we  support having this independent process  and whatever they decide is backdated to  the start of the year so I think it's a  fair process a lot of people have  remarked on how strange it is that even  as a lot of people are facing this you  know winter of discontent to squeeze on  their earnings at the same time you're  cutting the relative rate of Taxation  for for banks uh you're kind of pulling  down various restrictions and  regulations you announced them just last  Friday does that not look a little bit  awkward to say the least not at all  because what we want to do is to have  the fastest growing most prosperous  economy in Europe financial services are  responsible for about 10 of our economic  output a much higher proportion of the  taxes that pay for the NHS and public  services and if we want to fund those  Public Services we need to encourage our  high growth sectors whether it's  technology whether it's green Industries  whether it's life sciences and that's  what we'll be outlining over the months  ahead how worried final question how  worried you about the state of the labor  markets at the moment you know the UK  does look like it's in a kind of  stickier position than many other  countries around the G7 does that  concern you well we have a tight labor  market here but we're not the only  country to do that and certainly that's  something we'll be looking at in the  months ahead okay Chancellor thank you  very much indeed thank you  I think the treasury looks like it could  do with a couple of hundred watt bulbs  as you can see I'm joined Now by our  economics editor Ed Conway at our  business presenter Ian King I mean Ed to  you first let's just let's just talk  about the strikes and and public sector  pay I mean when you hear people like  Jeremy Hunt like Steve Barkley saying  that what the unions are asking for is  it is unaffordable there's just not  enough money to do what they want that's  nonsense isn't it it would be expensive  but it's nonsense to say that they don't  have the money to do that no I mean they  they could Finance you know quite a lot  more spending if they wanted to the  question is kind of what the quid pro  quo for that is and it's worth also  saying that you know even if you're  spending more money on on those wages  some of that would potentially come back  through higher tax take and some of it  would come back through higher spending  but the bigger picture I mean there's  two things there's first of all the  question of you know are they nervous  about the state of the public finances  now in the wake of what happened in the  midi budget certainly they are and that  kind of Inc declines to what the  treasury generally kind of feels it's  generally very conservative about this  stuff but secondly and more importantly  and you kind of got a sense of that from  from the interview there they are really  worried about inflation and they're  worried about kind of adding to that to  that pressure and the fear that that  haunts them in those slightly kind of  spooky looking uh rooms in the treasury  the fear that haunts them is of what  happened in the 1970s when you had this  interaction between wages and prices so  inflation went really high then people  kind of got the higher wages and that in  turn fueled the next round of inflation  and it became kind of inescapable you  just couldn't kind of put it back in the  box so that's what they say but the  reality is as you would have seen there  is that there is a big gap between the  private sector right now where wages are  running away really quite fast and the  public sector where they're not and as a  result you know you can make a good case  I mean it's difficult to compare those  two things it's difficult to compare  public sector earnings and private  sector earnings given there's all these  things like final salary pensions that  make you know it's more attractive in  many parts of the public sector but  definitely the private sector has been  catching up if you want to put it like  that like that way maybe overtaking and  here we are in this really tough period  both for public services for the rest of  the economy those numbers this morning  not looking very good  and so it is quite hard you know no one  is going to be happy out of these  negotiations but as you say it's not  quite as straightforward as saying we  can't afford it it's it's more complex  than that which is why I think the  government's struggling  um Ian just a word then from you on  those those GDP figures we are seeing  growth but but still think we're heading  into recession  well that seems to be the likelihood  yeah I mean the October number was uh  unexpectedly strong actually uh 0.5  growth better than the market was  looking for that I think largely  reflects the fact that there was a  bounce back for September there was an  extra bank holiday for the late Queen's  funeral and accordingly GDP came off  very sharply in September if you look at  the three months to October as a whole  UK economy contracted by some point  three percent uh that was uh that was  from the previous three months uh to the  end of July that's actually the worst  three monthly period since the first  quarter of January last year when of  course the UK was still in a covid  lockdown so that the broader picture i